1.2. More than four residents in ten live in Monte-Carlo and La Rousse
The Principality’s limited territory has an average population density of 18,681 inhabitants per square
kilometre.
Monaco is administratively divided into ordered districts, each with its own specific characteristics. The size
of the resident population varies greatly from one area to another.
Table 1. Number of residents by district in 2025
Source: Monaco Statistics - Population censuses
Monte-Carlo has more than 8,300 inhabitants, followed by La Rousse with nearly 8,000 residents. Together,
these districts account for more than four residents in ten.
Figure 3. Breakdown of residents by district in 2025
Note: The districts are those defined by Sovereign Order No. 4,481 of 13 September 2013. Ravin Sainte-Dévote has been
incorporated into the district of Les Moneghetti.
Source: Monaco Statistics - Population censuses
With just over 1,000 inhabitants, Monaco -Ville is the least populated district in the Principality (2.6%),
followed by Larvotto (2,287 residents, or 5.9% of the population).
La Condamine and Jardin Exotique districts have 5,446 and 4,997 residents respectively, or 14.0% and
12.9% of the population. Les Moneghetti and Fontvieille, for their part, have relatively similar populations,
with 4,498 and 4,297 residents, a little over 11% each.
