1.7. The growth of international communities over the censuses
Changes in the population over time result from various phenomena, the main ones being the natural
balance (the difference between the number of births and deaths over the period) and net migration (the
difference between arrivals and departures in the ter ritory). In terms of statistics by nationality, the
acquisition of Monegasque nationality through naturalisation or marriage may also play a role.
Figure 9. Change in the most represented nationalities from 1908 to 2025
Note: Some residents have more than one nationality. In that case, they are counted in as many communities as they
have nationalities. On the other hand, Monegasques, whether they have a single nationality or several, are only counted
as Monegasque. As a result, the total of the shares presented is greater than 100%.
Source: Monaco Statistics - Population censuses
Monegasques have been the leading nationality represented in the Principality since 2023, reaching 24.0%
of the population in 2025. Historically a minority, with fewer than one resident in ten between 1908 and
1938, their share has increased steadily since then.
By contrast, the share of French residents, which accounted for nearly 60% of residents in 1962, has
continued to decline and now stands at 21.3% in 2025. The proportion of Italians has remained relatively
stable over the long term, now reaching 19.5%.
